<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>CBL Operators and Case Studies</h2>

<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL is useful when it is simple to deploy, conservative in the flat limit,
and strictly stronger on instances with curved cores. We present two
drop-in operators—<span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> and <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span>—and three case studies
(SAT, CSP/AC, 3-Colorability). Each operator is sound, proof-loggable, and
costs essentially nothing on flat instances.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.1 </span>Two drop-in operators: <span id="S10.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> and <span id="S10.SS1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span></h3>

<section id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> (Curved Arc-Consistency).</h5>

<div id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given variable domains <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{D(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> and a context family <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> (each context
<math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> carries local constraints and overlaps), <span id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> eliminates
a value <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in D(x)</annotation></semantics></math> if there exists a minimal contextual face <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> in the overlap graph
such that every local assignment consistent with <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x{=}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x{=}v</annotation></semantics></math> violates the CBL overlap rules
on <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span><span id="algorithm2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> (Curved Arc-Consistency)</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.6.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm2.6.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm2.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Domains <math id="algorithm2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{D(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, contexts <math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, overlap map
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm2.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Pruned domains and certificates
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              Initialize FIFO <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> with all <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>; mark all unvisited
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm2.6.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="algorithm2.6.4.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> not empty</em> <span id="algorithm2.6.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              pop <math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,v)</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm2.6.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <em id="algorithm2.6.6.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in D(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="algorithm2.6.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsBlockedByCurvedFace<math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x{=}v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">v</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x{=}v)</annotation></semantics></math></span></em> <span id="algorithm2.6.6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5</span></span>
              eliminate <math id="algorithm2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(x)</annotation></semantics></math>; emit certificate <math id="algorithm2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{x,v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{x,v}</annotation></semantics></math> (dual check)
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">6</span></span>
              enqueue <math id="algorithm2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(y,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(y,w)</annotation></semantics></math> for all neighbors <math id="algorithm2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> sharing a context with <math id="algorithm2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">7</span></span>
              end if
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.6.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">8</span></span>
              end while
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.6.11" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> (Curved Overlap Consistency).</h5>

<div id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C,C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C,C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be overlapping contexts. If local proof engines derive
<math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\lnot\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\lnot\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot</annotation></semantics></math>, emit a global
contradiction on <math id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cup C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cup C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with a short dual certificate. This is the executable
form of the overlap transport rule (CONS) from Sec. <a href="#S7" title="7 Proof Theory ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm3" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 3</span> </span><span id="algorithm3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> (Curved Overlap Consistency)</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm3.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm3.6.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm3.6.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Local engines per context; overlap map
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.6.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.6.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm3.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Global cuts/certificates
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.6.3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.6.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
              <span id="algorithm3.6.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">foreach</span> <em id="algorithm3.6.3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">overlap edge <math id="algorithm3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,C^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,C^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math></em> <span id="algorithm3.6.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.6.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> 
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.6.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
              run local engines; if <math id="algorithm3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm3.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\lnot\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\lnot\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot</annotation></semantics></math>,
emit a global cut on <math id="algorithm3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cup C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cup C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with dual witness; record face ID;

</div>
<div id="algorithm3.6.5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm3.6.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
              end foreach
</div>
<div id="algorithm3.6.6" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Certificates.</h5>

<div id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each elimination or cut carries a short <em id="S10.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dual</em> certificate (linear/Boolean)
that we re-check in exact arithmetic. This guarantees soundness and supports
proof logging in SAT/CSP/MIP solvers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11 </span>Guarantees for <span id="S11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> and <span id="S11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span></h2>

<div id="Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Flat-limit conservativity)<span id="Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the instance is flat (no contextual faces), then <span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> reduces
to classical arc-consistency on the context graph, and <span id="Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span>
produces no cuts beyond overlap equalities.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text">With no faces, the overlap transport is trivial; there are no curved cores to
trigger blocking or overlap contradictions. The operators reduce to AC and
overlap equalities.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 7</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Strict dominance on curved cores)<span id="Thmtheorem7.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">On any instance with at least one minimal curved face, <span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> eliminates
at least one domain value that classical AC cannot (monotone improvement), or
<span id="Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> emits a global cut not derivable from single-context reasoning.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S11.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">On a minimal curved face, the parity/overlap pattern forces a local blockage on
one branch of a shared literal; classical AC does not propagate through the face
cycle and cannot see the joint obstruction. Overlap transport detects it.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 8</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Certificate soundness)<span id="Thmtheorem8.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Every elimination or cut emitted by <span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span>/<span id="Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> carries a
short dual witness (linear/Boolean) that verifies in exact arithmetic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S11.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Each blocked value/overlap contradiction arises from finitely many local constraints
on a face; their Farkas/Boolean dual compactly proves infeasibility.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem9" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 9</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(FPT in the number of curved faces)<span id="Thmtheorem9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If the instance has <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> curved faces and bounded overlap width,
search guided by <span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span>/<span id="Thmtheorem9.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> is FPT in <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (or reduces the exponential
base by a function of <math id="Thmtheorem9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S11.7" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S11.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S11.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">Branch only on a cover of faces; each face collapses by <span id="S11.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> or is
discharged by <span id="S11.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span>. The remaining flat part is handled by dynamic
programming over bounded overlap width.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">12 </span>Case Studies: SAT, CSP/AC, and 3-Colorability</h2>

<section id="S12.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.1 </span>SAT (structured families)</h3>

<div id="S12.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We integrate <span id="S12.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> into a CDCL solver as a proof-logged cut generator.
On structured families (Mermin/Feistel-style wirings, parity gadgets), curved
faces trigger early global cuts and certified UNSAT, reducing node counts by
<math id="S12.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S12.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80\%</annotation></semantics></math> on our prototypes. Flat instances incur near-zero overhead; proof
logs verify each cut in exact arithmetic.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.2 </span>CSPs via <span id="S12.SS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span> (Latin Square / Sudoku)</h3>

<div id="S12.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contexts are row/column/block scopes; overlaps are shared cells. <span id="S12.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-AC</span>
eliminates values that participate in curved faces formed by overlapping scopes
with parity/uniqueness constraints. We observe <math id="S12.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S12.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30\%</annotation></semantics></math> additional domain
pruning over classical AC before search on medium grids, with negligible overhead
on flat/easy puzzles.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S12.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">12.3 </span>3-Colorability (odd-cycle cores)</h3>

<div id="S12.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S12.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contexts are neighborhoods; curved faces are odd cycles with overlapping edges.
<span id="S12.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CBL-CONS</span> detects early infeasibility (3-color) by transporting contradictions
around odd cores and emitting global cuts. On planar triangulations and random graphs
with planted odd cores, we obtain early UNSAT with short certificates; DSATUR/backtracking
baselines do not see the contradiction without deeper search.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S13" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">13 </span>Noise Models and Robustness Guarantees</h2>

<div id="S13.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize three noise classes and state stability bounds for a generic CBL witness <math id="S13.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>
(e.g., a folded face statistic). Proofs and constants appear in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Expanded Proofs and Boundary-Case Analysis ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S13.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.1 </span>i.i.d. flips</h3>

<div id="S13.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each Bernoulli outcome is flipped independently with rate <math id="S13.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\in[0,1/2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\in[0,1/2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(i.i.d. stability)<span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz in Hamming distance on outcomes (true for the folded face
statistics we use). Under i.i.d. flips at rate <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S^{(\eta)}]\bigr|\leq\eta\,L_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S^{(\eta)}]\bigr|\leq\eta\,L_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{S}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{S}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, if Axiom A2 holds with <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\geq\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>η</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\geq\eta</annotation></semantics></math>, the deviation is <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.2 </span>AR(1) temporal correlation</h3>

<div id="S13.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Labels or outcomes undergo an AR(1)-type corruption along the time index with
correlation <math id="S13.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Correlated stability)<span id="Thmlemma2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Assume windowed folds of width <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math> with overlap <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\beta w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\beta w</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the effective flip rate concentrates around
<math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}=\eta\,\frac{1-\rho}{1+\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}=\eta\,\frac{1-\rho}{1+\rho}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bigl|\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S^{(\eta,\rho)}]\bigr|\leq\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}+O\!\left(\frac{\rho}{w}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mi>w</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bigl|\mathbb{E}[S]-\mathbb{E}[S^{(\eta,\rho)}]\bigr|\leq\eta_{\mathrm{eff}}+O\!\left(\frac{\rho}{w}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S13.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">13.3 </span>Adversarial bounded power</h3>

<div id="S13.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An adversary can modify at most a budget <math id="S13.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S13.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm on the vector of folded
counts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3</span></span><span id="Thmproposition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Worst-case bound)<span id="Thmproposition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\langle\mathbf{c},\mathbf{f}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\langle\mathbf{c},\mathbf{f}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear statistic of folded frequencies
<math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}\leq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}\leq\nu</annotation></semantics></math> (Axiom A2),
then <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S(\mathbf{f}+\Delta\mathbf{f})-S(\mathbf{f})|\leq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S(\mathbf{f}+\Delta\mathbf{f})-S(\mathbf{f})|\leq\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.
For Lipschitz-smooth nonlinear <math id="Thmproposition3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, the same holds up to first order, with curvature
controlled by Axiom A5.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S13.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counterexample (honesty).</h5>

<div id="S13.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S13.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If A3 fails (pathological long-range dependence), permutation nulls can be biased; a
synthetic construction with a block-permutation fix appears in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Expanded Proofs and Boundary-Case Analysis ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S14" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">14 </span>Evaluation Protocol</h2>

<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Instances.</h5>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) SAT families from Sec. <a href="#S12.SS1" title="12.1 SAT (structured families) ‣ 12 Case Studies: SAT, CSP/AC, and 3-Colorability ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12.1</span></a>, (2) Latin Square/Sudoku (sizes 9–25),
(3) 3-Colorability on planar/triangulated and random graphs (sizes <math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Metrics.</h5>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(1) Domain prunes vs. AC, (2) node counts/time vs. baselines (CDCL, DSATUR),
(3) number/size of certificates (dual checks), (4) wall-clock amortized overhead
on flat subsets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h5>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AC-3/AC-4 (CSP), Minisat/Glucose (SAT), DSATUR/backtracking (coloring).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reproducibility.</h5>

<div id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S14.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide code and proof logs for all cuts and eliminations; dual certificates
verify in exact arithmetic.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S15" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">15 </span>CBL for Large Language Models and Contextual AI Systems</h2>

<section id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Modern large language models (LLMs) exhibit context-sensitive reasoning: their outputs depend not only on the explicit prompt but also on latent priors and conversational history.
This behavior parallels the contextual dependence of measurement outcomes in quantum systems.
CBL provides a formalism for reasoning about such dependencies in a controlled, mathematically traceable way.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15.1 </span>Contextual stability in inference chains</h3>

<div id="S15.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An LLM’s response distribution can be viewed as a map</p>
<table id="S15.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R:(\text{prompt context},\text{latent state})\longrightarrow\text{token probabilities}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>prompt context</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>latent state</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mtext>token probabilities</mtext></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R:(\text{prompt context},\text{latent state})\longrightarrow\text{token probabilities}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Analogous to experimental observables, we can treat the prompt context as a “measurement setting” and the latent state as a hidden variable.
Under CBL, we introduce an <math id="S15.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded perturbation model:
small changes in the prompt or latent embedding must not change logical entailments beyond an <math id="S15.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> margin.</p>
</div>
<section id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Definition (CBL-stable inference).</h5>

<div id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An inference chain <math id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}\rightarrow c_{2}\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow c_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}\rightarrow c_{2}\rightarrow\dots\rightarrow c_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in an LLM is <em id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CBL-stable</em> if, for any perturbation <math id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> in the embedding space with <math id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta\|\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta\|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,
the resulting distributional change in the inferred logical relation remains bounded:</p>
<table id="S15.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\mathrm{KL}}(R(c_{i}+\delta)\,\|\,R(c_{i}))\leq f(\varepsilon)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>KL</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.337em">∥</mo><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{KL}}(R(c_{i}+\delta)\,\|\,R(c_{i}))\leq f(\varepsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S15.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> obeys the same Lipschitz bounds as in Axiom A2.
This captures deterministic robustness without requiring complete retraining.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15.2 </span>Counterfactual consistency for chain-of-thought reasoning</h3>

<div id="S15.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs generate reasoning traces (chains-of-thought) that can loop back on previous tokens.
CBL’s counterfactual semantics allows a consistent treatment of such loops:
a reasoning step may depend on hypothetical alternatives (<em id="S15.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">“what if this token were different”</em>)
without producing global contradictions.
In CBL notation, the inference graph of a multi-turn reasoning sequence becomes a folded contextual graph with curvature parameter <math id="S15.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Flat (<math id="S15.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}{=}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}{=}0</annotation></semantics></math>) reasoning corresponds to purely logical deduction; curved (<math id="S15.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}{\neq}0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}{\neq}0</annotation></semantics></math>) reasoning captures context reweighting, memory decay, or instruction alignment.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15.3 </span>Applications to alignment and interpretability</h3>

<section id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Alignment.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under bounded perturbations of prompt or reward signals, CBL yields quantitative <em id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alignment stability</em> metrics.
For instance, if a model’s instruction-following probability shifts by more than <math id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> under paraphrased prompts, it violates the <math id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded perturbation assumption and becomes context-unstable.
Such metrics can guide fine-tuning or reinforcement-learning-with-feedback loops.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretability.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each folded context in an LLM corresponds to a measurable projection of the attention map.
By treating attention heads as “measurement contexts” and activations as outcomes,
CBL quantifies contextual interference among heads.
The differential sensitivity <math id="S15.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dS/dp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dS/dp</annotation></semantics></math> then measures how much a local attention flip influences the global output,
offering a principled proxy for saliency.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15.4 </span>Algorithmic analogy</h3>

<div id="S15.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The connection between CBL-SAT pruning and LLM inference is direct:
both perform constraint satisfaction under limited consistency guarantees.
In CBL, curvature (<math id="S15.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>) modulates logical exclusivity;
in LLMs, attention softmax temperature plays a similar role,
controlling the degree of determinism versus contextual blending.
This analogy suggests practical compression and inference schemes in which
LLM decoding follows a CBL-consistent pruning logic rather than purely probabilistic sampling.</p>
</div>
<section id="S15.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL extends naturally from quantum contextuality to machine learning systems where reasoning is contextual, approximate, and perturbation-bounded.
Its <math id="S15.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stability and fold semantics provide a unified mathematical vocabulary for studying robustness, alignment, and interpretability in LLMs,
bridging foundational logic and practical AI inference.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">15.5 </span>CBL-Guided Compression and Adapter Stability</h3>

<section id="S15.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Motivation.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contemporary model-compression techniques—such as Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA),
quantization, and structured pruning—operate largely as engineering heuristics.
CBL provides a theoretical foundation for them by formalizing which
reductions preserve <em id="S15.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">contextual consistency</em>.
In this view, compression is not merely a loss of parameters but a
controlled deformation of the logical surface on which the model’s contextual
relations reside.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS5.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Compression as curvature minimization</h4>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> denote the full model and <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> its compressed surrogate.
Each layer implements a contextual mapping
<math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\ell}:\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbf{h}_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.108em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\ell}:\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1}\!\rightarrow\!\mathbf{h}_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Define the CBL curvature of layer <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S15.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{\ell}=\bigl\|\mathcal{C}_{\ell}(\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1}{+}\delta)-\mathcal{C}_{\ell}(\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1})\bigr\|\!/\!\|\delta\|," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" rspace="0.052em">/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{\ell}=\bigl\|\mathcal{C}_{\ell}(\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1}{+}\delta)-\mathcal{C}_{\ell}(\mathbf{h}_{\ell-1})\bigr\|\!/\!\|\delta\|,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">evaluated under bounded perturbations <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\delta\|\!\leq\!\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\delta\|\!\leq\!\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
A compression operator <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. rank-<math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> projection, quantization)
is <em id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CBL-admissible</em> if</p>
<table id="S15.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi^{\prime})-\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi)|\leq\varepsilon\quad\forall\ell." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi^{\prime})-\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi)|\leq\varepsilon\quad\forall\ell.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Minimizing total curvature drift
<math id="S15.SS5.SSSx1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{\ell}|\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi^{\prime})-\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{\ell}|\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi^{\prime})-\kappa_{\ell}(\Phi)|</annotation></semantics></math>
becomes an explicit objective during compression, ensuring that
contextual dependencies among layers are preserved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS5.SSSx2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Deterministic LoRA adaptation</h4>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Low-Rank Adapters inject matrices
<math id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=AB^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=AB^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> into frozen weights <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math>.
CBL interprets this as adding a controlled “curvature correction”:</p>
<table id="S15.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{\prime}=W+AB^{\top},\qquad\|\!AB^{\top}\!\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.580em;" width="0.580em"><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mpadded><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{\prime}=W+AB^{\top},\qquad\|\!AB^{\top}\!\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">guaranteeing <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded logical deviation of the output manifold.
This provides a deterministic criterion for adapter magnitude:
the adapter remains within CBL tolerance if its induced
<math id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies Axiom A2.
Empirically, this translates into bounded change of contextual response
distributions—measurable by <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dS/dp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dS/dp</annotation></semantics></math> between baseline and adapted models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS5.SSSx3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Compression as counterfactual equivalence</h4>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two models <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are <em id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">counterfactually equivalent</em>
up to <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> if, for any hypothetical prompt change <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta x</annotation></semantics></math>,
their induced reasoning trajectories differ by at most <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> in total variation:</p>
<table id="S15.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S15.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{\mathrm{TV}}\!\bigl(R_{\Phi}(x{+}\delta x),R_{\Phi^{\prime}}(x{+}\delta x)\bigr)\leq\varepsilon." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>TV</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\mathrm{TV}}\!\bigl(R_{\Phi}(x{+}\delta x),R_{\Phi^{\prime}}(x{+}\delta x)\bigr)\leq\varepsilon.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This mirrors the CBL requirement that perturbations do not change outcome
assignments beyond <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
Hence, model distillation or quantization can be certified as
CBL-consistent when the counterfactual equivalence bound holds.</p>
</div>
<section id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Operational impact.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Framing compression in CBL terms yields quantitative stability
certificates for deployment:</p>
<ul id="S15.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S15.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S15.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S15.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adapter safety:</span> verifies that LoRA/PEFT fine-tuning does not
introduce super-<math id="S15.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> contextual drift.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S15.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S15.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S15.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Compression auditing:</span> produces reproducible curvature
metrics analogous to physical calibration certificates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S15.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S15.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S15.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interoperability:</span> allows heterogeneous adapters to be
combined if their curvature deviations are additive within
global <math id="S15.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> budget.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL reframes compression and adaptation as geometric
operations on a contextual manifold, controlled by <math id="S15.SS5.SSSx3.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded
curvature drift.
This unifies model-robustness certification, adapter scaling, and logical
consistency under one theoretical umbrella and connects directly to
the patent-level framework for deterministic, loss-bounded data-compression
across both symbolic and neural substrates.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S16" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">16 </span>Experimental Feasibility, Power Analysis, and Figure Standards</h2>

<section id="S16.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">16.1 </span>Minimal feasible configuration</h3>

<div id="S16.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S16.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We enumerate detector efficiency, timing jitter, and pulse length ranges under which our folded statistics retain power:
(i) detector efficiency <math id="S16.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\eta_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\eta_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math> (table in supplement),
(ii) timing jitter <math id="S16.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq J_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq J_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> relative to window <math id="S16.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>,
(iii) context switching logged (A4).
We include a small calculator (supplement) that, given <math id="S16.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\eta,\rho,w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\eta,\rho,w)</annotation></semantics></math>, predicts the expected power curve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S16.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">16.2 </span>Power curves and robustness grid</h3>

<div id="S16.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S16.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We report empirical power for a grid <math id="S16.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\varepsilon,n,\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\varepsilon,n,\rho)</annotation></semantics></math> by simulation:
<math id="S16.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{10^{3},10^{4},10^{5}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>5</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{10^{3},10^{4},10^{5}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S16.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon\in\{0,0.01,0.05,0.1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon\in\{0,0.01,0.05,0.1\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S16.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in\{0,0.3,0.6\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.6</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in\{0,0.3,0.6\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each cell shows the discovery rate under BH at <math id="S16.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> (mean over <math id="S16.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> simulations).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S16.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">16.3 </span>Figure standards (mandatory)</h3>

<div id="S16.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S16.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every figure presenting a test must show:
(1) the observed curve,
(2) a shaded permutation null band (2.5–97.5%),
(3) corrected <math id="S16.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-values for any scan points called significant,
(4) an effect-size panel or value,
(5) seed, window <math id="S16.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, step <math id="S16.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S16.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> permutations in the caption.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S17" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">17 </span>Figure Standards and Visualization Protocol</h2>

<section id="S17.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Purpose.</h5>

<div id="S17.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section standardizes every figure in the manuscript to ensure statistical transparency, reproducibility, and referee-ready clarity.
All figures display null-band confidence intervals, corrected <math id="S17.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-values, explicit parameter settings, and deterministic seeds.
These conventions supersede any earlier figure formatting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">17.1 </span>Formatting and Conventions</h3>

<section id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual encoding.</h5>

<div id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Observed statistics are drawn as solid black curves.
Permutation-based null intervals (2.5–97.5 percentiles) appear as light-gray shaded bands.
Corrected <math id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-values from the Benjamini–Hochberg (BH) procedure are shown as red markers or secondary traces.
Numerical effect sizes <math id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-values are reported in captions.
Every caption explicitly states the parameters <math id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w,s,B,\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w,s,B,\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> and the random seed.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Required caption elements.</h5>

<div id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each caption contains:
(a) figure title summarizing the test or statistic;
(b) description of observed and null elements (color/line style);
(c) significance threshold and effect size;
(d) complete experimental parameters and seed;
(e) reference to the reproducibility artifact in Table <a href="#S20.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ Reproducibility checklist. ‣ 20 Statistical Procedures, Multiple-Testing Control, and Reproducibility ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Implementation note.</h5>

<div id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All figures are generated by the Colab notebook implementing Algorithm <a href="#algorithm4" title="In Null bands for figures. ‣ 20 Statistical Procedures, Multiple-Testing Control, and Reproducibility ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
The plotting routine computes the null band by <math id="S17.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}5000</annotation></semantics></math> permutations unless stated otherwise.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">17.2 </span>Example 1 — <math id="S17.SS2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-Stability Curve</h3>

<div id="S17.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection illustrates how the local perturbation sensitivity <math id="S17.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dS/dp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dS/dp</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated
from synthetic runs, highlighting the <math id="S17.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded robustness guaranteed by Axiom A2.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S17.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04716v1/epsilon_stability.svg" id="S17.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/304;" width="405" height="304"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S17.F8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S17.F8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Local <math id="S17.F8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stability of folded statistic.<span id="S17.F8.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Black curve = observed <math id="S17.F8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="dS/dp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">dS/dp</annotation></semantics></math>; gray band = 95% permutation-null interval (<math id="S17.F8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}5000</annotation></semantics></math>).
Red markers denote discoveries with corrected <math id="S17.F8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q{&lt;}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q{&lt;}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>; effect size <math id="S17.F8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta{=}0.27" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.27</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta{=}0.27</annotation></semantics></math>.
Parameters: <math id="S17.F8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w{=}256" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>256</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w{=}256</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S17.F8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s{=}16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s{=}16</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S17.F8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S17.F8.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">seed: 183921</span>.
All data and code available in artifact <span id="S17.F8.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">stable_curve.ipynb</span>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">17.3 </span>Example 2 — Folded Lag Scan</h3>

<div id="S17.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This example shows how lag-dependent contextual statistics reveal temporal correlations and how permutation-based null bands quantify their significance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S17.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04716v1/fold_lag_scan.svg" id="S17.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/322;" width="429" height="322"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S17.F9.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S17.F9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Lag-dependent contextual statistic.<span id="S17.F9.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Upper panel: observed statistic <math id="S17.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> (solid) and permutation-null mean (dashed) with 95% gray band.
Lower panel: corrected <math id="S17.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-values after BH at <math id="S17.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>; significant lags (<math id="S17.F9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q{&lt;}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q{&lt;}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) shaded red.
Run parameters: <math id="S17.F9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w{=}512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w{=}512</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S17.F9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s{=}32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s{=}32</annotation></semantics></math>, permutations <math id="S17.F9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B{=}5000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B{=}5000</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S17.F9.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">seed: 99041</span>.
Reproduction script: <span id="S17.F9.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">fold_lag_scan.ipynb</span>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S17.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">17.4 </span>Example 3 — Power and Robustness Grid</h3>

<div id="S17.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S17.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection presents the empirical discovery power across sample size <math id="S17.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and correlation
<math id="S17.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>, demonstrating statistical sensitivity and correlation tolerance of the CBL procedure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S17.F10" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04716v1/power_grid.png" id="S17.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/286;" width="381" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S17.F10.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S17.F10.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Power and robustness grid.<span id="S17.F10.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">
Empirical detection power under BH at <math id="S17.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha{=}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha{=}0.05</annotation></semantics></math> vs <math id="S17.F10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S17.F10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math>; mean over 200 replicates.
Contours: 0.2, 0.5, 0.8 power. Seeds: <span id="S17.F10.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[4001--4200]</span>. Artifact: <span id="S17.F10.7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">power_grid.ipynb</span>.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
