<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Road to publication</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This manuscript is first released on arXiv to stake priority and solicit feedback.
Candidate venues: <em id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Quantum</em> (foundations and computation), LICS (logic/complexity), QPL/TQC (category-theory and quantum logic).
Applications-oriented follow-ups may target SAT/verification conferences or ML workshops.
Audience: researchers in quantum foundations, logic in computer science, SAT/CSP, and AI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Call for collaboration</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL is at an early stage: semantics and solver prototypes are defined, but empirical validation and integration into mainstream solvers remain open.
We invite researchers from logic, SAT/CSP, verification, ML/AI, and quantum foundations to collaborate.
Open-source code and benchmarks will be released to foster a community effort.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">30 </span>Broader Implications and Potential Applications of CBL</h2>

<section id="S30.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Beyond quantum foundations.</h5>

<div id="S30.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While CBL emerged from the need to formalize contextuality and counterfactual consistency in quantum experiments,
its mathematical structure—Boolean curvature under bounded perturbations—appears in many areas of theoretical science.
This section outlines domains where CBL principles could provide new analytical traction on problems that have resisted classical methods.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.1 </span>Combinatorial and Complexity-Theoretic Applications</h3>

<div id="S30.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Classical logic treats satisfiability and exclusivity as discrete properties,
but many hard combinatorial problems hide continuous
“almost satisfiable” regimes that CBL can model through its curvature parameter <math id="S30.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Potential targets include:</p>
<ul id="S30.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S30.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S30.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S30.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph coloring and exclusivity bounds:</span> curvature relaxations may bridge chromatic and Lovász <math id="S30.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> gaps.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S30.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S30.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S30.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S30.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-SAT phase transitions:</span> the <math id="S30.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-stability parameter acts as an analytic order parameter describing the SAT–UNSAT boundary.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S30.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S30.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S30.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Spin-glass and Max-Cut landscapes:</span> frustration patterns become curvature loops; curvature rank predicts metastable basin counts.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.2 </span>Number Theory and Algebraic Structures</h3>

<div id="S30.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Non-associative or residue-based systems often break distributivity, the same axiom that CBL relaxes.
Possible venues include additive combinatorics, polynomial identity testing via deterministic <math id="S30.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-perturbations,
and analysis of octonionic or other non-distributive algebras through finite curvature embeddings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.3 </span>Logic and Proof Complexity</h3>

<div id="S30.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL introduces a geometric measure of contextual reuse.
This can be interpreted as a “curvature complexity” giving lower bounds on resolution or circuit size:
each contextual loop adds quantifiable logical tension that classical proofs must unwind step by step.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.4 </span>Probability, Statistics, and Information Theory</h3>

<div id="S30.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">CBL’s noise model (Axiom A2) formalizes adversarial rather than purely stochastic uncertainty.
This enables deterministic capacity bounds for correlated or worst-case channels,
and robust Bayesian updates whose posterior drift is bounded by <math id="S30.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> even under contextual dataset shifts.
In causal inference, folded CBL graphs extend ordinary DAGs to settings with latent feedback or self-reference,
maintaining identifiability conditions that fail in standard frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.5 </span>Geometry and Topology</h3>

<div id="S30.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The curvature operator of CBL connects naturally to discrete Ricci-curvature and graph-entropy formulations.
This unifies several competing notions of graph curvature
(Ollivier, Bakry–Émery, Forman) under a single counterfactual metric.
CBL’s folded constraints also resemble aperiodic tilings: adjacency rules become curved Boolean clauses,
suggesting algorithmic bounds for minimal non-periodic patches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.6 </span>Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence</h3>

<div id="S30.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In contextual AI systems, especially large language models,
CBL formalizes reasoning under bounded perturbations of prompts, embeddings, or parameters
(see Sec. <a href="#S15" title="15 CBL for Large Language Models and Contextual AI Systems ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> and <a href="#S15.SS5" title="15.5 CBL-Guided Compression and Adapter Stability ‣ 15 CBL for Large Language Models and Contextual AI Systems ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15.5</span></a>).
The same formalism underlies deterministic adapter certification,
continual-learning stability metrics, and generalization bounds for non-i.i.d. data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S30.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">30.7 </span>Summary and Outlook</h3>

<div id="S30.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these examples suggest that CBL is not a niche reformulation of quantum logic
but a general calculus for <em id="S30.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning under contextual tension</em>.
By translating contextual inconsistency into measurable curvature and <math id="S30.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-bounded deformation,
CBL supplies a unifying language for areas as diverse as complexity theory,
information geometry, causal inference, and AI alignment.
Its promise lies less in replacing existing tools than in making visible the geometric structure
that many “hard” problems have long shared but could not yet articulate.</p>
</div>
<section id="S30.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and code availability.</h5>

<div id="S30.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S30.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All figures and numerical results are produced by the open, Colab-ready
notebook <span id="S30.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">cbl_perm_bh_notebook.ipynb</span>, archived together with
the manuscript. Parameters and seeds are provided in
<span id="S30.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">params_example.json</span>, ensuring bit-level reproducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Expanded Proofs and Boundary-Case Analysis</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Worst-case bound). ‣ 13.3 Adversarial bounded power ‣ 13 Noise Models and Robustness Guarantees ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(\mathbf{f})=\langle\mathbf{c},\mathbf{f}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(\mathbf{f})=\langle\mathbf{c},\mathbf{f}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then by Cauchy–Schwarz,
<math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\langle\mathbf{c},\Delta\mathbf{f}\rangle|\leq\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}\leq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐜</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐜</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\langle\mathbf{c},\Delta\mathbf{f}\rangle|\leq\|\mathbf{c}\|_{2}\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}\leq\nu</annotation></semantics></math>,
so <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S(\mathbf{f}+\Delta\mathbf{f})-S(\mathbf{f})|\leq\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ν</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S(\mathbf{f}+\Delta\mathbf{f})-S(\mathbf{f})|\leq\nu</annotation></semantics></math>.
For smooth <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a first-order Taylor expansion with remainder
and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\nabla S\|_{2}\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\nabla S\|_{2}\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> yields the same bound up to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\|\Delta\mathbf{f}\|_{2}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>,
controlled by A5.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">B Proof sketches</h2>

<section id="Ax1.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.1 Variational rigidity: an extremal characterization of <math id="Ax1.SSx1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that <math id="Ax1.SSx1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> can be selected as the unique minimizer of a convex uniform error
over admissible gate laws, removing calibration by an internal optimality principle.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Admissible family.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the class of admissible CBL maps <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> (disk-preserving,
Boolean corners, flat limit, De Morgan).
For a minimal face family <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}=\{\mathrm{CHSH},\mathrm{KCBS},\mathrm{SAT6}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>CHSH</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>KCBS</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>SAT6</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}=\{\mathrm{CHSH},\mathrm{KCBS},\mathrm{SAT6}\}</annotation></semantics></math>),
let <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(n)}_{L}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(n)}_{L}(F)</annotation></semantics></math> denote the signed per-edge holonomy around a boundary loop
of length <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> under gate law <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Uniform error functional.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}(\theta;F):=\sup_{n\in\mathcal{B}}\,\sup_{L\geq L_{0}}\bigl|\,h^{(n)}_{L}(F)-\theta\,\rho^{(n)}_{\mathrm{face}}\,\bigr|,\qquad L_{0}\text{ large enough.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.0835em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.0835em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>face</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> large enough.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}(\theta;F):=\sup_{n\in\mathcal{B}}\,\sup_{L\geq L_{0}}\bigl|\,h^{(n)}_{L}(F)-\theta\,\rho^{(n)}_{\mathrm{face}}\,\bigr|,\qquad L_{0}\text{ large enough.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Then take the worst-admissible profile
<math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta):=\sup_{F\in\mathcal{F}}\mathcal{E}(\theta;F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta):=\sup_{F\in\mathcal{F}}\mathcal{E}(\theta;F)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Convexity and uniqueness in <math id="Thmlemma3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmlemma3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is convex in <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> and admits a unique minimizer
<math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\star}\in\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\star}\in\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">For fixed <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n,L,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n,L,F)</annotation></semantics></math> the map
<math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\mapsto|h^{(n)}_{L}(F)-\theta\,\rho^{(n)}_{\mathrm{face}}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>face</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\mapsto|h^{(n)}_{L}(F)-\theta\,\rho^{(n)}_{\mathrm{face}}|</annotation></semantics></math>
is convex.
Pointwise suprema preserve convexity; standard arguments yield existence and uniqueness
of a minimizer.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem10" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 10</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Variational rigidity fixes the scale)<span id="Thmtheorem10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> minimize <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\star}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}=\theta_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}=\theta_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> is independent of the basis <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math>
(provided <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> spans the NGCC class) and of the admissible gate law <math id="Thmtheorem10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consequently,</span></p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\mathrm{CBL}}=|\theta_{0}|\,\rho_{\mathrm{face}}/(2\pi)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>CBL</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>face</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\mathrm{CBL}}=|\theta_{0}|\,\rho_{\mathrm{face}}/(2\pi)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem10.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">is fixed without calibration.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Sketch.</h6>
<div id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Independence of <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> follows from the <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{F\in\mathcal{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mo>sup</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{F\in\mathcal{F}}</annotation></semantics></math> and uniqueness in
Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Convexity and uniqueness in 𝜃). ‣ Uniform error functional. ‣ B.1 Variational rigidity: an extremal characterization of 𝜃_0 ‣ B Proof sketches ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Changing the basis <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> within the same NGCC class changes only finitely many
constraints; strict convexity of <math id="Ax1.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> keeps the minimizer invariant.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 4</span></span><span id="Thmremark4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Operational use)<span id="Thmremark4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In practice, one approximates <math id="Thmremark4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the maximal tail-fit residual
across CHSH/KCBS/SAT families under any admissible implementation; stability of the
minimizer across implementations certifies rigidity.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.2 Soundness (Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Soundness). ‣ 7.3 Soundness ‣ 7 Proof Theory ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Local rules are sound by classical semantics.
For (OVL), if <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma\vdash_{C}\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma\vdash_{C}\psi</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Vars}(\psi)\subseteq C\cap C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Vars</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∩</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Vars}(\psi)\subseteq C\cap C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>,
then compatibility ensures <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{C^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{C^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> agrees, so <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ψ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi</annotation></semantics></math> holds in <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For (CONS), if <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\psi\vdash_{C}\bot</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma,\neg\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo rspace="0em">⊢</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">⊥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma,\neg\psi\vdash_{C^{\prime}}\bot</annotation></semantics></math>,
then any global assignment would contradict itself on <math id="Ax1.SSx2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\cup C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\cup C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.3 Flat-limit conservativity (Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Flat-limit conservativity). ‣ 7.4 Flat-limit conservativity ‣ 7 Proof Theory ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Ax1.SSx3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0</annotation></semantics></math>, every compatible family extends to a global valuation;
thus derivability in CBL coincides with Boolean derivability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.4 Constructive flattening for <math id="Ax1.SSx4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa=0</annotation></semantics></math>, pick a context <math id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and assignment <math id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{C_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{C_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Propagate values through overlaps; compatibility guarantees no contradictions.
Result: a global assignment <math id="Ax1.SSx4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g:V\to\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g:V\to\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.5 Worked flattening example</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Example: <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=\{x,y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=\{x,y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=\{x,y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=\{x,y\}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=\{y,z\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=\{y,z\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Constraints: <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\vee y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\vee y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y\vee z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∨</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y\vee z</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\neg x\vee\neg z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\neg x\vee\neg z</annotation></semantics></math>.
Assignment <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{C_{1}}(x,y)=(T,F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{C_{1}}(x,y)=(T,F)</annotation></semantics></math> propagates <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=F</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, requiring <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Global assignment <math id="Ax1.SSx5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(T,F,T)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(T,F,T)</annotation></semantics></math> works.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.6 Mermin-square UNSAT derivation</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each row/column enforces parity: product = <math id="Ax1.SSx6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>, except final column = <math id="Ax1.SSx6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Multiplying rows gives <math id="Ax1.SSx6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1</annotation></semantics></math>, multiplying columns gives <math id="Ax1.SSx6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Contradiction → no global assignment exists.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.7 Worked overlap propagation (KCBS)</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contexts <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}=\{v_{1},v_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}=\{v_{1},v_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}=\{v_{2},v_{3}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}=\{v_{2},v_{3}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Gamma=\{v_{1}=T\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma=\{v_{1}=T\}</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=F</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Rule (OVL) propagates <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{2}=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{2}=F</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, forcing <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{3}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{3}=T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Continuing around the cycle eventually forces <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}=F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}=F</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting <math id="Ax1.SSx7.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.8 Group-theoretic proof sketch of the vertex-turning law (Prop. <a href="#Thmproposition3" title="Proposition 3 (Worst-case bound). ‣ 13.3 Adversarial bounded power ‣ 13 Noise Models and Robustness Guarantees ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model CBL transport on the Poincaré disk via <math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> Möbius isometries.
Every <math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>PSU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> admits a Cartan (KAK) decomposition
<math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=K(\phi)A(\tau)K(\psi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=K(\phi)A(\tau)K(\psi)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> is a disk rotation (compact part) and <math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math> a hyperbolic boost.
Rotational factors compose additively: <math id="Ax1.SSx8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\theta_{2})K(\theta_{1})=K(\theta_{1}+\theta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\theta_{2})K(\theta_{1})=K(\theta_{1}+\theta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Corner transport.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At a face corner <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> the overlap rules (OVL/CONS), De Morgan duality, and the fiber
inversion for NOT (a <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> rotation) determine a local transport
(Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Convexity and uniqueness in 𝜃). ‣ Uniform error functional. ‣ B.1 Variational rigidity: an extremal characterization of 𝜃_0 ‣ B Proof sketches ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>):</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{v}=K(\delta_{v})\,A(\tau_{v})\,K(\psi_{v})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{v}=K(\delta_{v})\,A(\tau_{v})\,K(\psi_{v}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{v}</annotation></semantics></math> depending only on the logical pattern at <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> (not on edge lengths).
For minimal cores the corner pattern is isomorphic, so <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{v}\equiv\delta_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>≡</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{v}\equiv\delta_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> across
all corners.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Loop composition and central twist.</h5>

<div id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a minimal face with corners <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The total transport around <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial P_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial P_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\partial P_{n}}=K(n\delta_{n})\,A(\tau_{\Sigma})\,K(\Psi^{\prime})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\partial P_{n}}=K(n\delta_{n})\,A(\tau_{\Sigma})\,K(\Psi^{\prime}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The NGCC obstruction asserts that parallel transport around a minimal contextual
loop carries one unit of central fiber twist (<math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> phase), modulo a vertex-local gauge <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(n\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(n\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="Ax1.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="Ax1.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{\partial P_{n}}=K(2\pi)\,K(n\delta)\,A(\tau_{0})\,K(\Psi_{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{\partial P_{n}}=K(2\pi)\,K(n\delta)\,A(\tau_{0})\,K(\Psi_{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Comparing the <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-parts gives
<math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\delta_{n}=n\delta+2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\delta_{n}=n\delta+2\pi</annotation></semantics></math>, hence the vertex-turning law <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta_{n}=\delta+2\pi/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta_{n}=\delta+2\pi/n</annotation></semantics></math>,
and face area <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}=n\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}=n\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, independent of <math id="Ax1.SSx8.SSS0.Px2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 5</span></span><span id="Thmremark5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Conjugation by <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> rotates the boost axis but preserves rapidity:
<math id="Thmremark5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(\varphi)A(\tau)K(-\varphi)=\tilde{A}_{\varphi}(\tau)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>φ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>φ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(\varphi)A(\tau)K(-\varphi)=\tilde{A}_{\varphi}(\tau)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Along a closed <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-gon the boosts align pairwise by face closure,
so their net <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>-part reduces (or is absorbed into local gauge <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K(n\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K(n\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>).
All boosts are conjugate in <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>PSU</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{PSU}(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math>; the remaining abelian piece does not
affect the accumulated <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-part that fixes the central <math id="Thmremark5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi</annotation></semantics></math> twist.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Ax1.SSx9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">B.9 Variational rigidity (summary)</h3>

<div id="Ax1.SSx9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Ax1.SSx9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Combining Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Convexity and uniqueness in 𝜃). ‣ Uniform error functional. ‣ B.1 Variational rigidity: an extremal characterization of 𝜃_0 ‣ B Proof sketches ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and
Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem10" title="Theorem 10 (Variational rigidity fixes the scale). ‣ Uniform error functional. ‣ B.1 Variational rigidity: an extremal characterization of 𝜃_0 ‣ B Proof sketches ‣ Curved Boolean Logic: A Contextual Generalization of Propositional Logic with Algorithmic Consequences" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>
proves that the CBL coupling is fixed by internal convex minimization
without external calibration, closing the logical loop between geometry and algebra.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Toy benchmark generator</h2>

